"unfamiliar" and "dangerous" were often genuinely correlated, because strangers really were rare and often were, in fact, from rival groups. Applying that same wiring to a globalized, pluralistic society, where "unfamiliar to me" just means "different religion in my neighborhood," isn't the same problem the instinct was built for. It's less "ancient wisdom serving us well" and more "a tool being used outside the conditions it was calibrated for." **And the historical record actually argues against "instinct has served us fine."** Go back through the earlier examples — instinctive suspicion of Irish immigrants, Jewish communities, Japanese-Americans (leading to internment), Black Americans — in every case, people at the time felt exactly the way you're describing: this is just natural, obvious sizing-up, it's always worked, don't overthink it. In hindsight, those instincts are now almost universally recognized as having been wrong and harmful. "It felt reliable at the time" turned out to be a bad predictor of "it actually was reliable." So it's less that instinct is worthless, and more that this *specific kind* of instinct — snap category-based threat judgment about an unfamiliar identity group — is exactly the kind human instinct is worst at, and history backs that up pretty consistently.
